    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new function slots into the pipeline cleanly and mirrors its siblings well: `FlagPossibleDuplicate` is the exact structural twin of `AssessBulkSignup`/`CountNamesakes` (same `service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)` shape, assigns a flag onto the entity), it's wired after `assessBulkSignup` and before `save` with matching `govern`, the persisted columns across all four SQL dialects follow the `namesake_count`/`bulk_signup_warning` precedent, and the OpenAPI `readOnly` fields copy the `bulkSignupWarning` pattern. Logic is in the right unit, and the Javadoc is appropriately thorough. The issues are small duplication/idiom points, not placement.\n\nFindings:\n\n1. **`normalize` is a verbatim copy of `CountNamesakes.normalize`.**\n   `FlagPossibleDuplicate.java:58-60` reproduces exactly:\n   ```java\n   private static String normalize(String value) {\n       return value == null ? \"\" : value.trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);\n   }\n   ```\n   which is identical to `CountNamesakes.java:29-31`. This codebase has a clear convention of small shared static helper classes for owner-field normalization (`OwnerEmails`, `OwnerTelephones`, `OwnerAddresses`, `Households`). Last-name normalization now has two consumers (`CountNamesakes` and `FlagPossibleDuplicate`), so it should live in a shared helper (e.g. an `OwnerNames`/`OwnerLastNames` util, or a package-private static reused the way `Households.normalize` already is) rather than being copy-pasted into each step. A maintainer fixing normalization rules would otherwise have to find and change both copies.\n\n2. **`equalsTelephone` reinvents `java.util.Objects.equals`.**\n   `FlagPossibleDuplicate.java:54-56`'s null-safe string equality is exactly `Objects.equals(a, b)`. Dropping the private helper in favor of `java.util.Objects.equals(telephone, existing.getTelephone())` removes a custom method for a standard-library primitive and reads more naturally to a maintainer here.\n\nNeither affects where the logic lives \u2014 both are local cleanups within the otherwise well-placed new class.",
